<article class="env-definition" id="0AZV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AZV">Definition <span data-tag="0AZV">43.15.1</span>.</a> In the situation above, assume $d \geq \dim (\text{Supp}(M))$. In this case, if $d > \dim (\text{Supp}(M))$, then we set $e_ I(M, d) = 0$ and if $d = \dim (\text{Supp}(M))$, then we set $e_ I(M, d)$ equal to $d!$ times the leading coefficient of the numerical polynomial $\chi _{I, M}$. Thus in both cases we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \chi _{I, M}(n) \sim e_ I(M, d) \frac{n^ d}{d!} + \text{lower order terms}  \]
</div>
  <p> The <em>multiplicity of $M$ for the ideal of definition $I$</em> is $e_ I(M) = e_ I(M, \dim (\text{Supp}(M)))$. </p>
</article>